WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.140 --> 00:00:03.780
- Philip, our savings is gone!

00:00:03.780 --> 00:00:04.830
Look!

00:00:04.830 --> 00:00:05.663
- Yeah, I know.

00:00:05.663 --> 00:00:06.810
I put it in the stock market.

00:00:06.810 --> 00:00:08.850
- Now? While the market's tanking?

00:00:08.850 --> 00:00:09.990
What were you thinking?

00:00:09.990 --> 00:00:10.920
- You told me to.

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:12.240
- No, I didn't.

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:13.073
- Yeah, you did.

00:00:13.073 --> 00:00:14.017
You texted me yesterday,

00:00:14.017 --> 00:00:16.110
"Don't forget to buy the dip."

00:00:16.110 --> 00:00:20.040
- I meant the onion dip
for the party this weekend.

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:21.277
- Oh.

00:00:21.277 --> 00:00:24.420
- (sighs) I hope you at
least bought some blue chips.

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:25.424
- Of course I did.

00:00:25.424 --> 00:00:26.967
(bag crinkles)

00:00:26.967 --> 00:00:28.293
- Well, that's corny.

00:00:29.750 --> 00:00:32.333
(upbeat music)

00:00:38.250 --> 00:00:39.720
Whenever the market is down,

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:41.790
you'll hear a chorus of voices saying

00:00:41.790 --> 00:00:43.740
you should "Buy the dip."

00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:45.810
But what does that mean exactly?

00:00:45.810 --> 00:00:47.490
- Put simply, buying the dip

00:00:47.490 --> 00:00:49.740
means investing money
into the stock market

00:00:49.740 --> 00:00:51.900
when share prices have recently fallen.

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:54.210
Since the shares are essentially on sale,

00:00:54.210 --> 00:00:57.960
you get more bang for your
buck, or so the thinking goes.

00:00:57.960 --> 00:00:59.310
When the market rises again,

00:00:59.310 --> 00:01:01.530
you'll get a higher
return on your investment.

00:01:01.530 --> 00:01:03.180
If the goal of every stock trader

00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:05.550
is to buy low and sell high,

00:01:05.550 --> 00:01:08.160
then buying the dip makes total sense.

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:10.950
- Imagine, for instance, that
this is the last 12 months

00:01:10.950 --> 00:01:12.390
of a blue chip stock price.

00:01:12.390 --> 00:01:13.710
If you get it-

00:01:13.710 --> 00:01:15.333
- Oh, blue chip!

00:01:16.544 --> 00:01:18.247
- (sighs) If you manage to buy, say,

00:01:18.247 --> 00:01:20.790
$1,000 worth of stock at this point,

00:01:20.790 --> 00:01:24.390
you would've almost doubled
your money in less than a year.

00:01:24.390 --> 00:01:27.180
- But, of course, nothing
looks easier in hindsight

00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:28.410
than stock trading.

00:01:28.410 --> 00:01:30.510
The hard part is knowing when a stock

00:01:30.510 --> 00:01:32.280
has hit its lowest point.

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:34.440
If you had bought it just a bit earlier,

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:36.330
you wouldn't be making nearly as much.

00:01:36.330 --> 00:01:38.370
And if you waited just a bit longer,

00:01:38.370 --> 00:01:40.440
you would've missed
your chance completely.

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:43.020
- And that's just the
beginning of the dangers.

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:46.350
Buying the dip relies on a
statistical principle called

00:01:46.350 --> 00:01:48.480
regression towards the mean.

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:51.870
It says that ups and downs
are basically temporary,

00:01:51.870 --> 00:01:53.310
and they will all average out

00:01:53.310 --> 00:01:56.130
to reflect a stable statistical trend.

00:01:56.130 --> 00:01:57.480
Traders who buy the dip

00:01:57.480 --> 00:02:00.780
are assuming that if a
stock price falls sharply,

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:03.420
it will most likely go back up again.

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:05.610
- And that is the case much of the time,

00:02:05.610 --> 00:02:07.800
especially when fluctuations are based

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:10.050
on investor emotional trends.

00:02:10.050 --> 00:02:12.300
But sometimes, a fall in stock price

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:14.550
is indicative of a fundamental problem

00:02:14.550 --> 00:02:16.470
in the company or sector.

00:02:16.470 --> 00:02:18.180
Maybe a company is poorly run

00:02:18.180 --> 00:02:20.370
and the stock price deserves to crash.

00:02:20.370 --> 00:02:21.690
Instead of buying the dip,

00:02:21.690 --> 00:02:23.940
you may find yourself
tumbling down a cliff

00:02:23.940 --> 00:02:25.500
with no bottom in sight.

00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:26.790
- Despite these risks,

00:02:26.790 --> 00:02:29.310
you still might be
interested in buying the dip.

00:02:29.310 --> 00:02:31.350
So, how should you go about it?

00:02:31.350 --> 00:02:34.560
Most investors who attempt
it will set some portion

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:37.200
of their investment dollars aside in cash,

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:39.870
waiting for a stock to
fall from a recent peak

00:02:39.870 --> 00:02:44.070
by a certain percentage,
say between 10 and 50%.

00:02:44.070 --> 00:02:46.380
Both ends of this spectrum carry risk.

00:02:46.380 --> 00:02:48.240
If you set the percentage too low,

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:51.404
you may buy in well before
a stock has hit its bottom.

00:02:51.404 --> 00:02:55.260
But set it too high, and you
may be waiting around forever.

00:02:55.260 --> 00:02:59.100
- For example, Ted here has
about $20,000 to invest,

00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:01.230
but he wants to buy the dip.

00:03:01.230 --> 00:03:03.660
So he keeps $3,000 in cash

00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:07.560
and waits for one of his
favorite stocks to fall by 20%.

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:10.110
Finally, after 12 months, it happens.

00:03:10.110 --> 00:03:12.690
And Ted picks up 20% more shares

00:03:12.690 --> 00:03:14.970
than his money would've
bought the week before.

00:03:14.970 --> 00:03:17.220
The stock falls a little lower. Eek!

00:03:17.220 --> 00:03:19.500
But then starts regressing
toward the mean,

00:03:19.500 --> 00:03:21.810
even surpassing its recent high.

00:03:21.810 --> 00:03:24.630
Well done, Ted! You beat the system.

00:03:24.630 --> 00:03:25.800
- Or, did he?

00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:29.100
Ted may have bought the stock
at a relatively low price.

00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:31.590
But when you look a little
further back in time,

00:03:31.590 --> 00:03:34.200
it doesn't seem like such a win.

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:38.460
This stock, like many, has a
fairly consistent upward trend.

00:03:38.460 --> 00:03:41.400
He bought the stock for less
than it cost a week ago,

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:44.250
but more than it cost 12 months ago.

00:03:44.250 --> 00:03:47.700
If Ted had just gotten all of
his money in at the beginning,

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:49.500
he'd have done better overall.

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:52.800
- Ted failed to factor
in the opportunity cost

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:54.570
of waiting to buy the dip.

00:03:54.570 --> 00:03:56.580
Most financial advisors will recommend

00:03:56.580 --> 00:03:59.670
that if you have money
to invest, invest it now.

00:03:59.670 --> 00:04:01.320
Cash that just sits around

00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:03.210
is worse than wasted potential,

00:04:03.210 --> 00:04:06.540
it's actually losing
value thanks to inflation.

00:04:06.540 --> 00:04:09.000
- There is, however,
another investment method

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:11.670
that confers some of the
benefits of buying the dip

00:04:11.670 --> 00:04:13.380
without all of the risks.

00:04:13.380 --> 00:04:15.960
It's called dollar-cost averaging.

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:17.640
It's actually really simple.

00:04:17.640 --> 00:04:18.473
You just commit

00:04:18.473 --> 00:04:20.790
to putting the same
percentage of your paycheck

00:04:20.790 --> 00:04:23.010
into the stock market every month,

00:04:23.010 --> 00:04:25.200
regardless of whether it's up or down.

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:27.720
- So for instance, let's
say that Marisol here

00:04:27.720 --> 00:04:31.530
decides to put $200 a month
into her favorite stocks,

00:04:31.530 --> 00:04:33.000
no matter the price.

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:35.460
That means that when the
share price is inflated,

00:04:35.460 --> 00:04:36.960
she'll buy less of them.

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:39.647
And when the share price
is low, she'll buy more.

00:04:39.647 --> 00:04:42.690
It's an automatic way of
making sure you're paying

00:04:42.690 --> 00:04:45.180
what the stock is really
worth historically.

00:04:45.180 --> 00:04:47.670
And for the vast majority of investors,

00:04:47.670 --> 00:04:49.770
it has a much better track record

00:04:49.770 --> 00:04:51.223
than trying to buy the dip.

00:04:51.223 --> 00:04:52.320
- Don't get us wrong.

00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:55.530
Some people have had great
success in buying the dip.

00:04:55.530 --> 00:04:56.700
And if you've been waiting

00:04:56.700 --> 00:04:58.920
for the right time to start investing,

00:04:58.920 --> 00:05:01.770
it's not a bad idea to get
in when shares are cheap.

00:05:01.770 --> 00:05:05.100
- But just like a gambler who
occasionally hits the jackpot,

00:05:05.100 --> 00:05:06.960
most people who make a habit of it

00:05:06.960 --> 00:05:09.060
will eventually come out behind.

00:05:09.060 --> 00:05:10.980
Study after study has concluded

00:05:10.980 --> 00:05:14.610
that accurately timing the
market is virtually impossible.

00:05:14.610 --> 00:05:17.220
- Disciplined investors
who think long-term

00:05:17.220 --> 00:05:18.960
and don't try to beat the system

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:21.660
almost always perform better.

00:05:21.660 --> 00:05:23.955
- [Together] And that's our two cents.

00:05:23.955 --> 00:05:25.140
- History lovers,

00:05:25.140 --> 00:05:27.690
